---
import '../styles/global.css';

const masterclass = 'https://michael-borck.github.io/the-human-edge/';
const cnd = 'https://conversationnotdelegation.com';
const books = 'https://books.borck.education';
const consulting = 'https://borck.consulting';
const email = 'mailto:michael@borck.dev?subject=The%20Human%20Edge';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Book',
  name: 'The Human Edge: Using AI with Judgement',
  author: {
    '@type': 'Person',
    name: 'Michael Borck',
    url: 'https://borck.dev',
  },
  url: 'https://thehumanedge.borck.dev',
  image: 'https://thehumanedge.borck.dev/cover.png',
  description:
    'When everyone has the same AI tools, generic competence is the baseline. The edge is judgement: knowing what to ask, when to trust the answer, and where a person stays in charge.',
  inLanguage: 'en-AU',
  creativeWorkStatus: 'In development',
};
---
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en-AU">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
  <title>The Human Edge · Using AI with Judgement</title>
  <meta name="description" content="When everyone has the same AI tools, generic competence is the baseline. The edge is judgement. A book and masterclass by Michael Borck." />
  <link rel="canonical" href="https://thehumanedge.borck.dev/" />
  <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg" />
  <meta name="theme-color" content="#f5e6c8" />
  <meta property="og:title" content="The Human Edge · Using AI with Judgement" />
  <meta property="og:description" content="Everyone has the same AI tools now. The edge is judgement." />
  <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
  <meta property="og:url" content="https://thehumanedge.borck.dev/" />
  <meta property="og:site_name" content="The Human Edge" />
  <meta property="og:image" content="https://thehumanedge.borck.dev/og.png" />
  <meta property="og:image:width" content="1200" />
  <meta property="og:image:height" content="630" />
  <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" />
  <meta name="twitter:image" content="https://thehumanedge.borck.dev/og.png" />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />
</head>
<body>

<header class="topbar">
  <a class="wordmark" href="/">The Human <span>Edge</span></a>
  <nav class="nav">
    <a href={masterclass}>The masterclass</a>
    <a href={cnd}>The method</a>
    <a href={books}>Books</a>
    <a href={consulting}>Consulting</a>
  </nav>
</header>

<main>

  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">Judgement / Workplace AI</p>
      <h1>The Human <span>Edge</span></h1>
      <p class="subtitle">Using AI with Judgement</p>
      <p class="lead">Everyone has the same AI tools now. The edge is judgement.</p>
      <p class="body">
        Generic competence used to be an advantage. It is now the baseline. This book
        and masterclass are about the part AI cannot manufacture: knowing what to ask,
        deciding how much to trust the answer, checking it from outside the tool, and
        keeping a person in charge where the stakes require it.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="btn" href={masterclass}>See the masterclass</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={email}>Talk about a workshop</a>
      </div>
      <p class="status">The book is in manuscript. The one-day masterclass is running.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-cover">
      <img
        src="/cover.png"
        alt="The Human Edge: Using AI with Judgement by Michael Borck"
        width="600"
        height="900"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="pullquote">
    <p>AI can do the work. It cannot carry the consequence.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <p class="section-kicker">The problem</p>
    <h2>Fluent output is not the same as good work.</h2>
    <div class="split">
      <p>
        A report arrives. It is fluent, well structured, and complete. So does a data
        brief, a proposal, and a plan for a difficult conversation. Any professional
        with Copilot, ChatGPT, Claude, or Gemini can now produce work that looks like
        this, in minutes.
      </p>
      <p>
        The failure is hard to spot. A rushed human draft looks rushed, and the gaps
        show. An AI output that is wrong arrives polished, complete, and confident, in
        the same tone as an output that is right. The instinct you have spent your
        career building stays quiet exactly when you need it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <p class="section-kicker">The four moves</p>
    <h2>Judge. Brief. Check. Deliver.</h2>
    <div class="grid">
      <article class="card">
        <p class="card-num">01</p>
        <h3>Judge</h3>
        <p class="card-tool">Trust Tool</p>
        <p>How much should I trust this output? A ten-second habit for every output.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <p class="card-num">02</p>
        <h3>Brief</h3>
        <p class="card-tool">RTCF</p>
        <p>How do I ask for what I need? Build a prompt library that holds up.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <p class="card-num">03</p>
        <h3>Check</h3>
        <p class="card-tool">Check Loop</p>
        <p>How do I know it is right? Verify the claim from outside the tool.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <p class="card-num">04</p>
        <h3>Deliver</h3>
        <p class="card-tool">Opportunity Finder</p>
        <p>Will it work every week? Put it into a real workflow with human checkpoints.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <p class="section-kicker">Research basis</p>
    <h2>Why the transfer works.</h2>
    <p class="section-lead">
      The argument draws on peer-reviewed research on cognitive strategy transfer.
      The paper explains why human complexity-management strategies improve LLM
      output, and where the transfer breaks down. It is conceptual research, not a
      study of consulting outcomes.
    </p>
    <p class="citation">
      Borck, M. (2026). <em>Cognitive Strategy Transfer: Why Human Complexity-Management
      Strategies Improve Large Language Model Performance</em>. Accepted at the
      Australasian Conference on Information Systems (ACIS 2026), Perth, Australia.
    </p>
    <a class="text-link" href="https://research.borck.dev/#cognitive-strategy-transfer">Read the research →</a>
    <p class="disclosure">
      This research was conducted in my academic role. The practice is independent
      and not endorsed by Curtin University.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <p class="section-kicker">The method underneath</p>
    <h2>Conversation, not delegation.</h2>
    <p class="section-lead">
      The Human Edge is the workplace book. Conversation, Not Delegation is the method
      underneath it: the discipline of thinking with AI instead of handing the thinking
      over. The four moves in this book are that method applied to the work you sign
      your name to.
    </p>
    <a class="text-link" href={cnd}>Read the framework →</a>
  </section>

  <section class="section team">
    <div>
      <p class="section-kicker">For teams</p>
      <h2>Bring the masterclass to your team.</h2>
      <p class="section-lead">
        A one-day program built from the book. Four sessions, eight exercises, four
        reusable tools. It is for professionals who need to decide what AI should do,
        what a person must still own, and how to explain that choice to a client or a
        regulator.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="btn" href={masterclass}>See the masterclass</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={email}>Email Michael</a>
    </div>
  </section>

</main>

<footer class="footer">
  <p>
    Michael Borck · Perth, Western Australia ·
    <a href="mailto:michael@borck.dev">michael@borck.dev</a>
  </p>
  <p class="footer-links">
    <a href={cnd}>Conversation, Not Delegation</a>
    <a href={books}>Books</a>
    <a href={consulting}>borck.consulting</a>
    <a href="https://borck.dev">borck.dev</a>
  </p>
</footer>

</body>
</html>
